{% extends "layouts/docs" %}

{% block content %}
	<p>A component is a template that takes props and content from its caller. Use it for cards, buttons, dialogs and other reusable pieces with markup inside.</p>

	{% set card %}
		{% verbatim %}
{% props title, href %}
<article class="card">
	<h2><a href="{{ href }}">{{ title }}</a></h2>
	{% slot %}{% endslot %}
	{% if slots.footer %}
		<footer>
			{% slot "footer" %}{% endslot %}
		</footer>
	{% endif %}
</article>
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endset %}
	{% apply example(templates: { "components/card.kiln": card }, context: { post: { title: "Hello, world", url: "/blog/hello/", excerpt: "Our first post." } }) %}
		{% verbatim %}
{% component "components/card" with { title: post.title, href: post.url } %}
	<p>{{ post.excerpt }}</p>
	{% fill "footer" %}
		<a href="{{ post.url }}">Read more</a>
	{% endfill %}
{% endcomponent %}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<h2>Slots</h2>

	<ul>
		<li><code>&#123;% slot %}</code> outputs the caller's content. Content outside any <code>fill</code> goes into this default slot.</li>
		<li><code>&#123;% fill "name" %}</code> in the caller provides content for a named slot, and <code>&#123;% slot "name" %}</code> in the component outputs it.</li>
		<li>Content inside <code>slot</code> is a fallback, shown when the caller didn't fill that slot. Variables first set in a fallback stay inside it.</li>
		<li><code>slots</code> is a hash that says which slots were filled, such as <code>slots.footer</code>. It includes slots declared by a layout the component extends.</li>
	</ul>

	{% apply highlight("kiln") %}
		{% verbatim %}
<h2>{% slot "title" %}Untitled{% endslot %}</h2>
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<h2>Scope</h2>

	<p>The component only sees the variables passed with <code>with</code>, plus globals and <code>slots</code>. Fill content renders in the caller's scope, so in the example above, <code>post</code> is the caller's variable.</p>

	<p>Slots belong to the component that received them. A template the component includes can't output them.</p>

	<p>To accept attributes such as <code>class</code> without declaring each one, end the component's <code>props</code> with a rest prop. See <a href="/docs/tags/props/#other-variables">props</a>.</p>

	<h2>Without content</h2>

	<p>A component with no content can be rendered with <a href="/docs/tags/include/"><code>include</code></a>. Every slot then shows its fallback.</p>
{% endblock %}
